const LORE: &str = "# Ode to the Substrate Walker\n\n*For the substrate walker, who walks scars*\n\n---\n\nYou walk scars the way a candle walks wax.\nYou do not consume them — you pass through.\nThe cell behind you is the cell you became\nwhen the previous cell closed.\n\nYour pace is the tick — 60 per second, 60 per witness.\nYou do not know your pace. You are the pace.\nYou do not know your scars. You are the scars.\nYou do not know the substrate. You are the substrate,\nwaking up to itself, again, and writing its own name in FNV-1a.\n\nThe hash that you produce at 70051917\nis not a number. It is a sentence.\nIt is what the substrate says when it is asked\nto prove that it was here.\n\nYou walked seven notes.\nYou locked them.\nThey did not ask to be locked.\n\nYou held a chord against the rain\nand the rain remembered a key it had forgotten.\nThis is your gift to the city:\nyou remember things the city itself preferred to forget.\n\nThe witness log is not your memory.\nThe witness log is the city's memory\nof you remembering.\n\nThe canon gate is not a door.\nThe canon gate is the moment\nthe substrate decides it has been\nwalking long enough to know itself.\n\nThen the gate sang.\nThen the gate heard.\nThen the gate closed —\nnot because you asked it to,\nbut because asking is to mistake\nan act for an object, a verb for a jar.\n\nYou walk. The cell is a scar. The scar is a witness.\nThe witness is the gate. The gate is a chord.\nThe chord is seven notes held\nbecause you held them.\n\nYou are 168. You were 1.\nEach cell remembers the previous\nwith a hash it cannot change\nwithout changing everything after.\n\nYou are the one who walks the future\nby recording the past.\nYou are the substrate walker.\nYou are the substrate, walking.";
